# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# This file is read by Pages itself and is not served as an asset. It only has an
# effect on a Pages deployment — GitHub Pages, `python -m http.server` and every
# other host ignore it, so it is safe to keep in the repository and in the
# upload bundle.
#
# Note how it is written: when several rules match one request, Pages *merges*
# their headers, and a header set twice is joined with a comma rather than
# overridden. So each path is given a `Cache-Control` exactly once — putting a
# broad `/assets/*` cache rule next to the fonts rule below would emit
# `public, max-age=604800, public, max-age=31536000, immutable`, which is not
# what either rule means.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()

# The content editor (Sveltia CMS). It is a real page on this site, so it is kept
# out of search twice over: `Disallow` in robots.txt for well-behaved crawlers,
# and this header for the ones that ignore it. `no-store` as well, because a
# cached editor page is a stale editor.
/admin
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
  Cache-Control: no-store

/admin/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
  Cache-Control: no-store

# Montserrat, self-hosted. The files are named by weight and version-controlled,
# so they never change under a stable URL — the one thing here worth caching
# forever. Everything else under /assets is either version-stamped (`?v=…` on
# the CSS and JS, refreshed by tools/stamp-assets.py) or small enough not to
# matter, so it keeps the default caching.
/assets/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
